1
-1:59:59,960 --> 00:00:03,999
Two days after this speech,
the FBI marked its speaker as a threat.

2
00:00:04,581 --> 00:00:07,707
This is the story of the speech,
and of the memo it caused.

3
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:22,202
It starts in the spring of 1963,
in Birmingham, Alabama.

4
00:00:22,653 --> 00:00:27,729
Black residents there were protesting segregation,
and Martin Luther King was one of the leaders.

5
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:32,601
That spring the city saw violence,
including a bombing at the Gaston Motel,

6
00:00:33,052 --> 00:00:35,668
and a Library of Congress
photograph shows the damage.

7
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:41,308
Faced with the protests, President John F.
Kennedy decided to back a new civil rights law.

8
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:47,096
On 11 June 1963, he went on radio
and television to explain why.

9
00:00:47,687 --> 00:00:50,433
That same day, National
Guard troops had been needed

10
00:00:50,453 --> 00:00:53,509
to admit two Black students
to the University of Alabama.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,031
He told the country that a Black baby born
in America that day had about half the chance

12
00:00:59,051 --> 00:01:02,749
of finishing high school that a white
baby born the same day had.

13
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:04,675
Then he named the problem.

14
00:01:05,607 --> 00:01:08,717
We are confronted primarily with a moral issue.

15
00:01:09,107 --> 00:01:11,822
Eight days later, he sent his proposal to Congress.

16
00:01:12,353 --> 00:01:13,856
The National Archives notes

17
00:01:13,876 --> 00:01:18,273
that the message stressed how segregation
hurt the nation's standing in the world,

18
00:01:18,603 --> 00:01:20,096
in the middle of the Cold War.

19
00:01:20,627 --> 00:01:24,934
Civil rights leaders wanted to show Washington
how many people stood behind the bill.

20
00:01:25,507 --> 00:01:27,843
A. Philip Randolph, a veteran labor leader,

21
00:01:27,914 --> 00:01:31,935
had been calling for a march on Washington,
and in May King joined that call.

22
00:01:32,507 --> 00:01:36,188
Its official name was the March
on Washington for Jobs and Freedom.

23
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:38,892
It demanded a strong civil rights bill,

24
00:01:39,242 --> 00:01:43,416
protection of the vote, school desegregation,
and a federal jobs program.

25
00:01:43,967 --> 00:01:47,874
The date was 28 August, eleven
weeks after Kennedy's speech.

26
00:01:53,527 --> 00:02:00,723
On 28 August 1963, people poured into Washington
from all over the country, many of them by bus.

27
00:02:01,334 --> 00:02:04,380
The National Archives says
hundreds of thousands came.

28
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:06,687
They gathered at the Lincoln Memorial.

29
00:02:07,338 --> 00:02:11,456
History.com reports that the leaders
chose it, rather than the Capitol,

30
00:02:11,867 --> 00:02:14,562
so members of Congress would not feel under siege.

31
00:02:15,194 --> 00:02:18,785
A film crew from the United States
Information Agency recorded the day,

32
00:02:18,805 --> 00:02:20,901
and the film still has its sound.

33
00:02:21,494 --> 00:02:24,254
Singing ran through the program,
and so did speeches.

34
00:02:25,217 --> 00:02:27,667
We shall overcome

35
00:02:28,017 --> 00:02:29,667
some day.

36
00:02:30,427 --> 00:02:33,662
Another speaker talked about how
America would look from abroad.

37
00:02:34,423 --> 00:02:37,243
It's going to have its effect on the image

38
00:02:37,263 --> 00:02:40,433
of our country all over the world.

39
00:02:40,601 --> 00:02:42,646
Then a speaker singled out King.

40
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:46,530
Clearly, the religious leader of this occasion

41
00:02:46,550 --> 00:02:48,300
was Martin Luther King.

42
00:02:48,561 --> 00:02:51,967
A minister's prayer asked God
to bless the United States.

43
00:02:52,950 --> 00:02:55,530
Pour out Thy benediction

44
00:02:55,550 --> 00:03:00,400
upon the United States of America.

45
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:02,532
According to John Lewis's memoir,

46
00:03:02,662 --> 00:03:06,616
his own speech was toned down that day
after objections from other leaders.

47
00:03:07,307 --> 00:03:10,371
According to Clarence B. Jones,
King's lawyer and adviser,

48
00:03:10,391 --> 00:03:15,709
A. Philip Randolph argued that King should speak
last, because most people were coming to hear him.

49
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:20,968
Jones gave that account in a 2013 oral
history for the Library of Congress.

50
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,233
When King reached the microphones
at the Lincoln Memorial,

51
00:03:24,583 --> 00:03:27,116
he began by reading from his prepared text.

52
00:03:28,107 --> 00:03:32,017
Five score years ago,

53
00:03:37,367 --> 00:03:41,408
By his own account, King had finished
that text around three thirty that morning.

54
00:03:41,981 --> 00:03:44,105
A typescript had already gone to the press.

55
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:48,175
Jones says he drafted the first
six or seven paragraphs,

56
00:03:48,646 --> 00:03:52,902
including the passage about
a promissory note and insufficient funds.

57
00:03:53,534 --> 00:03:55,699
Here is King delivering that passage.

58
00:03:56,613 --> 00:04:00,153
...a bad check, a check which has come back

59
00:04:00,173 --> 00:04:02,383
marked "insufficient funds."

60
00:04:02,693 --> 00:04:07,287
Then, Jones says, the singer Mahalia
Jackson, who had performed earlier,

61
00:04:07,358 --> 00:04:09,629
called out to King to tell them about the dream.

62
00:04:10,181 --> 00:04:11,905
Here is Jones telling it himself.

63
00:04:12,730 --> 00:04:14,890
She had performed earlier on the program,

64
00:04:14,910 --> 00:04:16,150
"Tell them about the dream, Martin.

65
00:04:16,170 --> 00:04:17,400
Tell them about the dream."

66
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:22,414
Jones says King moved his text to the side
of the lectern and gripped the lectern,

67
00:04:22,685 --> 00:04:24,336
and that his whole manner changed.

68
00:04:24,887 --> 00:04:29,642
Jones says he turned to the person next to him
and said the crowd was about to go to church.

69
00:04:30,253 --> 00:04:33,969
King later recalled that he turned
aside from the manuscript altogether.

70
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:38,161
Other accounts agree that the dream
passage was not in the written text.

71
00:04:38,733 --> 00:04:40,645
But historians add a nuance.

72
00:04:41,216 --> 00:04:46,030
King had used the dream refrain before,
including in Detroit in June 1963.

73
00:04:46,521 --> 00:04:47,908
The words were not new to him.

74
00:04:47,928 --> 00:04:49,486
The moment was.

75
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:51,835
This is where the speech turns.

76
00:04:52,707 --> 00:04:55,247
...today and tomorrow,

77
00:04:55,267 --> 00:04:57,717
I still have a dream.

78
00:04:57,933 --> 00:05:02,429
He spoke of his four children, and of a country
that would judge them by their character.

79
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:06,780
...where they will not be judged
by the color of their skin,

80
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,950
but by the content of their character.

81
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:15,336
Jones says King looked at his notes again only
near the end, for the old hymn line, free at last.

82
00:05:16,217 --> 00:05:18,817
Free at last, free at last,

83
00:05:18,837 --> 00:05:21,767
thank God Almighty, we are free at last.

84
00:05:22,027 --> 00:05:25,103
That is about all of the speech this video can play.

85
00:05:25,894 --> 00:05:31,755
The National Archives says it is under copyright, so
a complete recording is not something we can show.

86
00:05:32,287 --> 00:05:34,973
The free government film of the day never reaches it.

87
00:05:35,387 --> 00:05:38,622
What it does catch is King afterwards,
talking to the camera.

88
00:05:39,493 --> 00:05:42,983
...of the greatest, if not the greatest demonstrations

89
00:05:43,273 --> 00:05:45,583
for freedom and human dignity

90
00:05:50,834 --> 00:05:55,755
After the march, King and the other leaders went
to the White House to meet President Kennedy.

91
00:05:56,287 --> 00:05:59,572
The next day, James Reston
wrote in the New York Times

92
00:05:59,592 --> 00:06:02,046
that King had touched all the themes of the day,

93
00:06:02,256 --> 00:06:03,889
only better than anybody else.

94
00:06:04,561 --> 00:06:08,307
Inside the FBI, a quiet fight
had been going on for weeks.

95
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:14,136
Hoover had already written on an FBI memo,
back in 1962, that King was no good.

96
00:06:14,627 --> 00:06:18,768
A Senate committee, led by Frank Church,
later published the paper trail.

97
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:20,693
In the week before the march,

98
00:06:20,884 --> 00:06:26,628
the bureau's domestic intelligence division sent
Director J. Edgar Hoover a sixty seven page study.

99
00:06:27,181 --> 00:06:32,248
It found that the Communist Party had mostly
failed to win influence among Black Americans.

100
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:34,172
Hoover disliked that answer.

101
00:06:34,543 --> 00:06:39,059
He wrote across it that it reminded him
of memos he had received about Fidel Castro,

102
00:06:39,470 --> 00:06:41,502
and that time had proved those analysts wrong.

103
00:06:42,054 --> 00:06:47,232
William Sullivan, who ran that division, later told
the Senate that his men feared for their jobs,

104
00:06:47,463 --> 00:06:49,396
and wanted a new memo to get out of trouble.

105
00:06:49,867 --> 00:06:54,288
He wrote it on 30 August 1963,
two days after the speech.

106
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:58,332
In it, Sullivan said that after King's
powerful demagogic speech,

107
00:06:58,763 --> 00:07:02,415
King stood head and shoulders above
all other Negro leaders put together

108
00:07:02,445 --> 00:07:04,356
in influencing great masses of people.

109
00:07:04,867 --> 00:07:10,535
He said the bureau must now mark King as the most
dangerous Negro of the future in this nation.

110
00:07:11,147 --> 00:07:13,267
That is the shock this story can prove.

111
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:19,462
In 1975, Sullivan told the Senate that he put
in that memo what Hoover wanted to hear.

112
00:07:20,014 --> 00:07:23,058
Sullivan also told the committee
that writing it meant a lot

113
00:07:23,078 --> 00:07:26,072
of nonsense that he and his men
did not really believe in.

114
00:07:26,562 --> 00:07:29,316
They either did that, he said,
or they would be finished.

115
00:07:29,827 --> 00:07:32,893
Either way, the memo shows
how the bureau read the speech.

116
00:07:33,527 --> 00:07:37,001
On 16 September, Sullivan asked
for more aggressive coverage.

117
00:07:37,412 --> 00:07:40,255
Hoover refused, asking how
he could switch so quickly,

118
00:07:40,275 --> 00:07:43,869
when only weeks before he had called
Communist influence ineffective.

119
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:50,393
In October, Attorney General Robert Kennedy
approved FBI wiretaps on King's home and offices.

120
00:07:50,944 --> 00:07:55,876
The committee could not establish why he said
yes after refusing the same request in July.

121
00:07:56,427 --> 00:08:01,628
In December, senior FBI officials met
to plan ways to neutralize King's influence.

122
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:05,185
Days later, Time magazine
named King Man of the Year,

123
00:08:05,205 --> 00:08:09,362
and Hoover said they had to dig deep
in the garbage to come up with that one.

124
00:08:09,894 --> 00:08:11,746
Meanwhile the law moved.

125
00:08:12,478 --> 00:08:17,356
President Johnson signed
the Civil Rights Act on 2 July 1964.

126
00:08:17,907 --> 00:08:20,027
King won the Nobel Peace Prize that year.

127
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:24,336
He received it in Oslo on 10 December 1964.

128
00:08:24,807 --> 00:08:29,404
In 1965, marchers walked from Selma
to Montgomery for the right to vote,

129
00:08:29,755 --> 00:08:32,549
and the Voting Rights Act was signed on 6 August.

130
00:08:33,180 --> 00:08:37,519
On 4 April 1968, King was shot
dead in Memphis, Tennessee.

131
00:08:38,050 --> 00:08:39,142
He was 39.

